From: 77450danik <dnsystems@email.ua>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] server: A negative coordinate keeps its sign when the DPI changes.

scale_dpi() multiplied the value by an unsigned ratio: a negative
coordinate became a large unsigned number, and the result about 2^32/96
instead of a few pixels to the left. Every rectangle the server maps from
a DPI-unaware window to the pixels of its monitor went through it: at 125 %
a maximized window, whose frame lies 4 pixels beyond the left and top edges
of the work area, was placed some 44 million pixels away. dwm.exe composes
windows from those rectangles, so the picture viewer, the "Open with"
dialog and any program that does not know the DPI vanished when maximized,
or when moved partly beyond the left or top edge of the screen; GetWindowRect
asked by a DPI-aware program got the same numbers. The ratio is signed, the
product 64-bit.
---
diff --git a/server/user.h b/server/user.h
--- a/server/user.h
+++ b/server/user.h
@@ -237,9 +237,10 @@

 static inline int scale_dpi( int val, struct ratio dpi_from, struct ratio dpi_to )
 {
-    unsigned int num = dpi_to.num * dpi_from.den, den = dpi_from.num * dpi_to.den;
-    if (val >= 0) return (val * num + (den / 2)) / den;
-    return (val * num - (den / 2)) / den;
+    /* Arctic: signed, or a negative coordinate turns into a huge positive one */
+    long long num = dpi_to.num * dpi_from.den, den = dpi_from.num * dpi_to.den;
+    if (val >= 0) return (int)((val * num + (den / 2)) / den);
+    return (int)((val * num - (den / 2)) / den);
 }

 static inline void scale_dpi_rect( struct rectangle *rect, struct ratio dpi_from, struct ratio dpi_to )
